#: includes/admin.php:1656
msgid "My robots.txt is not delivered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1657
msgid "WordPress only delivers its robots.txt when no physical robots.txt file exists in the web root. If one exists, the web server delivers it directly and the plugin is never asked – the robots.txt tab shows the file and offers to rename it. Also check that the site is not set to \"Discourage search engines\" and that override files are readable by the web server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1662
msgid "I changed the content in the editor, but the old one is still delivered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1663
msgid "A file in the override directory takes precedence over your rules – delete it or copy it into your rules first. Otherwise it is almost always a cache: purge your page cache plugin, your CDN and the server cache, then reload /robots.txt in a private window."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1668
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1669
msgid "Modes and checks"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1673
msgid "What is the difference between \"Base\", \"Exact\" and \"Blocking\"?"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1674
msgid "In \"Base\" mode, your rules replace the WordPress default and SEO plugins may still change them: Yoast SEO removes the WordPress default rules and appends its own rules and sitemap, All in One SEO rebuilds the robots.txt. In \"Exact\" mode, your rules are delivered as written, after all SEO plugins have run. The crawler options and the rules for sites with a path are added in both modes. \"Blocking\" mode, which can only be set per site, blocks all crawlers from all paths – for example for a staging site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1675
msgid "Base:     your rules, then SEO plugins may change them\nExact:    your rules as written, SEO plugins cannot change them\nBlocking: User-agent: * / Disallow: / for all crawlers"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1679
msgid "What does the status check?"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1680
msgid "It requests the robots.txt of the site as crawlers get it and shows whether all crawlers are blocked, how many sitemaps are linked and whether crawlers that collect content for AI training are blocked. It also reports when the robots.txt cannot be loaded, when a physical file is delivered and when the site is hidden from search engines. The result is kept for the time set in the settings."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1686
msgid "Compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1687
msgid "Setup and removal"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1691
msgid "Does it work if WordPress is installed in a subdirectory?"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1692
msgid "Crawlers only read the robots.txt at the root of a domain. If the address of the site contains a path (example.com/blog/), WordPress cannot deliver it there, so the plugin does nothing and says so. If only the WordPress files are in a subdirectory and the address is the root of the domain, it works."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1696
msgid "What happens on uninstall?"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1697
msgid "The settings and everything stored per site – rules, mode, crawler options and status – are deleted from the database, on every site of a network. Files on the server are never touched: override files and a renamed _old-robots.txt are kept."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1730
msgid "Need more help?"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:1731
msgid "Run the status check first – it detects most delivery problems automatically."
msgstr ""
